          "path": "src/app/api/studio-upload/route.ts",
          "line": 53,
          "severity": "P3",
          "title": "Ownership lookup error is discarded, so DB failures return a misleading 404",
          "text": "🟢 **P3**\n\n**Ownership lookup error is discarded, so DB failures return a misleading 404**\n\nThe ownership query destructures only `data` and drops `error`. If the lookup fails (network blip, PostgREST error, RLS misconfiguration), `ownedListing` is null and the caller gets \"Listing not found\" (404). The user is told the listing doesn't exist, and nothing is logged. This route already special-cases transient failures with the `AuthRetryableFetchError` retry above. The same pattern is in `handleAgentPost` and `handleAgentGet` in `src/app/api/agent/[listingId]/route.ts`. Consider destructuring `error`, logging it, and returning a 500 when it is set. Keep the uniform 404 only for the genuine no-row case."
        },
        {
          "id": "r0:c4430888168:0",
          "roundIndex": 0,
          "reviewId": 5721724219,
          "source": "inline",
          "commentId": 4430888168,
          "path": "src/app/api/listings/ownership-routes.test.ts",
          "line": 90,
          "severity": "P2",
          "title": "Comps \"non-owner\" tests don't model a listing owned by someone else",
          "text": "🟡 **P2**\n\n**Comps \"non-owner\" tests don't model a listing owned by someone else**\n\nThe comps handlers rely on RLS on the session client for ownership, but the fake Supabase has no RLS. For the non-owner case, `compsSetup(INTRUDER, false)` just empties the `listings` table, so the 404 comes from \"listing doesn't exist\", not \"listing belongs to another user\". If the handler's ownership/RLS-dependent lookup were weakened (for example, dropped a `user_id` filter), these tests would still pass. That makes them weaker than the other non-owner tests in this file, which seed a real row owned by `owner` and call as `intruder`. To keep the coverage meaningful, seed `listings: [{ id: 'l1', user_id: 'owner' }]` in the non-owner case. That only helps if the comps handler filters on `user_id` explicitly. If it relies purely on RLS, the test name or a comment should say so."
        }
      ]
    }
